Mail Transport
The script requires a working sendmail interface.


Option 1: msmtp-mta (lightweight SMTP relay)


Option 2: postfix (local MTA)


See examples/msmtprc.example.
Safety Notes


Default behavior may power off the node. Set POWER_OFF_NODE=0 if you do not want shutdown.


apt dist-upgrade can change kernels and require reboot; plan maintenance windows accordingly.


The emailed report includes the last 200 lines of the maintenance log; treat it as potentially sensitive.


License
Choose a license (MIT is common for scripts) and add it to LICENSE.

---

## 7) systemd service + timer (recommended for automation)

### `systemd/proxmox-maintenance.service`
```ini
[Unit]
Description=Proxmox Maintenance + Health Report
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/proxmox-maintenance
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/proxmox-maintenance.sh
Nice=10
IOSchedulingClass=best-effort
IOSchedulingPriority=7

# If you want to enforce root:
User=root
Group=root

systemd/proxmox-maintenance.timer
[Unit]
Description=Schedule Proxmox Maintenance + Health Report

[Timer]
OnCalendar=Sun *-*-* 03:00:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=300

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target

/etc/default/proxmox-maintenance (ENV file on the node)
MAIL_TO="you@example.com"
POWER_OFF_NODE=0
LOG_FILE="/var/log/proxmox_maintenance.log"
STATE_DIR="/var/lib/proxmox_maintenance"
HTML_REPORT="/tmp/proxmox_dashboard_report.html"

Enable:
install -m 0755 proxmox-maintenance.sh /usr/local/sbin/proxmox-maintenance.sh
install -m 0644 systemd/proxmox-maintenance.service /etc/systemd/system/proxmox-maintenance.service
install -m 0644 systemd/proxmox-maintenance.timer /etc/systemd/system/proxmox-maintenance.timer
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now proxxmox-maintenance.timer


8) msmtp example (lightweight sendmail provider)
examples/msmtprc.example
# ~/.msmtprc or /etc/msmtprc (choose one)
defaults
auth           on
tls            on
tls_trust_file /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
logfile        /var/log/msmtp.log

account        smtp
host           smtp.gmail.com
port           587
from           proxmox@example.com
user           proxmox@example.com
passwordeval   "cat /root/.secrets/msmtp_password"

account default : smtp

Permissions guidance:
chmod 600 /etc/msmtprc
chmod 600 /root/.secrets/msmtp_password

Note: If you publish an msmtp example, keep it credential-free and call out security best practices.

9) Release notes / “known limitations” you may want to document


VM shutdown is graceful (qm shutdown), but if VMs hang past VM_GLOBAL_WAIT, the script continues and may power off the node. (That is intentional in your workflow; document it.)


PBS detection:


With jq, it checks JSON state "running".


Without jq, it relies on textual “running” match, which is less robust.




SMART check enumerates /dev/sd? and /dev/nvme?n?. Some systems use other naming patterns (e.g., RAID controllers), and SMART passthrough may require additional flags; document that it is “best effort.”



10) What I recommend adjusting before public GitHub (non-breaking, but important)
Even if you keep your script as-is internally, for the public repo version you should strongly consider:


Replace your real MAIL_TO default with a placeholder (and rely on ENV for real value).


Add a conspicuous README warning about POWER_OFF_NODE=1 default.


Include a LICENSE (MIT, Apache-2.0, or GPL-3.0 are typical; MIT is common for small admin scripts).


If you want, paste your intended repository name and whether you prefer MIT vs Apache-2.0, and I will provide the exact LICENSE file text and a polished final README.md that matches your naming and usage style.
